course: dictionary
lesson: 4597
title: Hereby: words that make things happen
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Hereby is a formal word that you will find in laws, official notices and certificates. It means «by this statement, letter or document».
explain: Look at the clue in «I hereby declare the fete open.» The speaker is not just describing the fete. Saying these words makes it open, and hereby shows that the words themselves do the job.
explain: In everyday talk you would say «I now declare the fete open», but hereby sounds official. Grown-ups use it on paper and in ceremonies, and children sometimes use it playfully to sound important.
fact: Hereby means by this statement, letter or document.
fact: «I hereby declare» means that saying these words makes the declaration happen.
fact: «I hereby promise» means that the promise is made by saying or writing it.
fact: Hereby is used in formal writing such as certificates, contracts, court orders and old-fashioned speeches.
fact: Hereby is an adverb, so it adds information about how an action is done.
exercise: «The court hereby orders that the child shall reside with the applicant.» What does hereby mean here? | *By this document, the court makes the order | The judge is going out to lunch | The court is very tired and needs a rest | The court is closed for the day
exercise: Lexi says hereby is just a fancy word for now. What is the best answer? | Yes, it means very soon | Yes, it means exactly the same as now | No, it means underneath the table | *Not quite: hereby means by this statement, letter or document, so the words make it happen
exercise: Which sentence is most likely to use hereby? | *«I hereby declare the fete open.» | «It is raining again.» | «I am very hungry today.» | «Let's go to the park after school.»
test: In «Notice is hereby given that the matter shall be reviewed», what is hereby doing? | *It makes the notice official by saying it in this document | It shows the notice is wet from the rain | It is the name of a person | It tells us the notice is for a dog
test: Which is the most formal way to say I promise? | I promise, lol | Promise, sorry | *I hereby promise | I'm sure I promise, maybe
exam: Mia writes «I hereby certify that this is a true copy.» Why does she use hereby? | To mean that it is raining outside | To mean that she is sad about the copy | *To show that writing these words makes the certificate official | To show that she is cold
